@* The Razor side of the §7.1 allocation comparison. Must render frame-for-frame identically to
   BenchmarkView; Program.cs refuses to benchmark if it does not.

   Written on one line, with every text node coming from an expression. Newlines between tags become
   markup frames, and a literal element subtree is folded into a single AddMarkupContent frame, so
   the conventional multi-line form would not emit the same frames as the generator. See the remarks
   on BenchmarkView. *@
<div class="card"><h1>@Title</h1><span>@($"Count: {Count}")</span><p>@Description</p></div>

@code {
    public string Title { get; set; } = "Benchmark";

    public int Count { get; set; }

    public string Description { get; set; } = "A paragraph of content.";

    // Exposes the Razor compiler's render path on the same terms as BenchmarkView.Build.
    public void Build(RenderTreeBuilder builder) => BuildRenderTree(builder);

    // Counterpart to BenchmarkView.Invalidate; see the remarks there.
    public void Invalidate() => StateHasChanged();
}
